# =====================================================================================
#  02 - WORKING: API Management HTTP proxy + OAuth compatibility ("working setup")
#  /ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
#
#  The same steps as 01-not-working-native.http, in the same order. The differences:
#    step 1  the challenge points at metadata APIM serves itself
#    step 2  the PRM names APIM as the authorization server
#    step 3  APIM's authorization-server metadata (with a registration endpoint)
#    step 4  POST /register returns the pre-registered public client
#    step 5  APIM's /authorize replaces "resource" and redirects to Entra: no error
#    step 6  APIM's /token applies the same translation and returns the user's token
#  Steps 5 (browser) and 6 (code) are manual; see http/README.md.
#
#  Variables (environment file): gateway, org, tenant, clientId, token.
# =====================================================================================

# Fixed, lab-only PKCE pair (RFC 7636). Never reuse a fixed verifier in a real client.
@codeVerifier = lab-only-pkce-verifier-0123456789-abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
@codeChallenge = zaFp8oSoZN9fqMAEJGn4XTBBL02a9LeZix3gER7YRB4
@redirectUri = http://localhost
@mcpUrl = {{gateway}}/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
# Paste the "code" from the browser address bar after step 5 (manual, valid ~10 minutes).
@authCode = paste-the-code-here
# Token for steps 7-11. Default: the az token from your environment file. After a
# successful step 6, use {{token6.response.body.$.access_token}} instead.
@bearer = {{token}}

###
# STEP 1 - Anonymous initialize
# WHAT  : The first MCP message, without a token.
# WHY   : MCP authorization 2025-06-18: a 401 tells the client where the metadata is.
# EXPECT: HTTP 401, and (proxy-mcp.xml outbound replaced the origin's challenge)
#           WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://<gateway-host>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/ado-mcp-proxy/<org>", scope="https://mcp.dev.azure.com/.default"
#           Cache-Control: no-store, X-Correlation-ID, x-apim-request-id
#         Difference to native: the metadata URL is APIM's own document, and the
#         challenge also names the scope (MCP 2025-11-25 recommends it).
# LOGS  : policy-traces.kql steps proxy-inbound / proxy-outbound ("challenge points at
#         APIM-hosted metadata"); www-authenticate-rewrite.kql.
# @name init
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP 2 - Protected-resource metadata (served by oauth-prm.xml)
# WHAT  : The document named in the challenge, at the RFC 9728 path-inserted location.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "resource": "https://<gateway-host>/ado-mcp-proxy/<org>",   <- equals the URL the client uses
#             "authorization_servers": [ "https://<gateway-host>" ],      <- APIM, not Entra
#             "bearer_methods_supported": [ "header" ],
#             "scopes_supported": [ "https://mcp.dev.azure.com/.default" ],
#             "resource_name": "Azure DevOps MCP via API Management" }
#         Difference to native: the authorization server is the gateway, so the client
#         sends its authorize and token requests to APIM, which can translate them.
# LOGS  : client-signins.kql / policy-traces.kql, step prm.
# @name prm
GET {{gateway}}/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/ado-mcp-proxy/{{org}}
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP 3 - Authorization-server metadata (served by oauth-as-metadata.xml)
# WHAT  : RFC 8414 metadata of the authorization server from step 2 (response chaining).
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "issuer": "https://<gateway-host>",
#             "authorization_endpoint": "https://<gateway-host>/authorize",
#             "token_endpoint": "https://<gateway-host>/token",
#             "registration_endpoint": "https://<gateway-host>/register",
#             "code_challenge_methods_supported": [ "S256" ], ... }
#         issuer equals the URL it was fetched from (RFC 8414 section 3.3; Copilot CLI checks it).
# LOGS  : step as-metadata.
# @name asMetadata
GET {{prm.response.body.$.authorization_servers[0]}}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP 4 - Dynamic client registration (oauth-register.xml)
# WHAT  : The client registers itself, as most MCP clients do (RFC 7591).
# WHY   : Entra has no registration endpoint; APIM answers with the one pre-registered
#         public client and echoes the redirect URIs. Entra still enforces them.
# EXPECT: HTTP 201
#           { "client_id": "<public-client-id>", "client_name": "http-walkthrough",
#             "redirect_uris": [ "http://localhost" ], "token_endpoint_auth_method": "none", ... }
# LOGS  : client-signins.kql, step register (clientName, redirectUris).
# @name register
POST {{asMetadata.response.body.$.registration_endpoint}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"client_name":"http-walkthrough","redirect_uris":["{{redirectUri}}"],"grant_types":["authorization_code","refresh_token"],"response_types":["code"],"token_endpoint_auth_method":"none"}

###
# STEP 5 - Authorization request through APIM (oauth-authorize.xml)
# WHAT  : The same request as native step 5, but sent to APIM's /authorize, still with
#         resource = the gateway URL, exactly as a client builds it.
# WHY   : APIM removes "resource" and adds resource=https://mcp.dev.azure.com, the
#         identifier Entra accepts for the Azure DevOps scope. PKCE S256 is required.
# EXPECT: HTTP 302, Location:
#           https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/oauth2/v2.0/authorize?client_id=...
#             &redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost&scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
#             &code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256&state=walkthrough-proxy
#             &resource=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com          <- replaced
#         Difference to native: no invalid_target. Entra serves its sign-in page.
# MANUAL: Copy the Location value into a browser and sign in with an account of the
#         organization's tenant. The browser then goes to
#           http://localhost/?code=<long value>&state=walkthrough-proxy
#         and shows "can't connect": nothing listens on localhost, that is expected.
#         Copy the code value from the address bar into @authCode above.
# LOGS  : client-signins.kql, step authorize (redirectUri, clientResource = the gateway URL).
# @name authorize
# @no-redirect
GET {{asMetadata.response.body.$.authorization_endpoint}}
    ?client_id={{register.response.body.$.client_id}}
    &response_type=code
    &redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
    &scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
    &resource={{mcpUrl}}
    &code_challenge={{codeChallenge}}
    &code_challenge_method=S256
    &state=walkthrough-proxy

###
# STEP 6 - Token request through APIM (oauth-token.xml)  [needs @authCode from step 5]
# WHAT  : Exchanges the code for tokens (RFC 6749 section 4.1.3), with the PKCE verifier
#         and resource = the gateway URL, as a client sends it.
# WHY   : APIM replaces resource again and forwards the form to Entra's token endpoint.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "token_type": "Bearer", "scope": "https://mcp.dev.azure.com/Ado.Mcp.Tools ...",
#             "expires_in": 3600-ish, "access_token": "eyJ...", "refresh_token": "..." }
#         The access token's audience is Azure DevOps MCP and it belongs to the user.
#         Then set @bearer = {{token6.response.body.$.access_token}} above.
#         Without a real code (the placeholder) Entra answers 400 invalid_grant, AADSTS9002313;
#         an already used or expired code gives invalid_grant AADSTS54005 / AADSTS70008.
# SHORTCUT: skip steps 5-6 and use the az token from the environment file (@bearer = {{token}}).
# LOGS  : client-signins.kql, steps token and token-response (status only; tokens are never logged).
# @name token6
POST {{asMetadata.response.body.$.token_endpoint}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code
&client_id={{register.response.body.$.client_id}}
&code={{authCode}}
&redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
&code_verifier={{codeVerifier}}
&resource={{mcpUrl}}

###
# STEP 7 - Authenticated initialize
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, text/event-stream (REST Client shows the raw "event: message / data: {...}"
#         text), serverInfo AzureDevOps.Mcp, protocolVersion 2025-06-18. No Mcp-Session-Id.
#         X-Correlation-ID and x-apim-request-id come back for log lookups.
# LOGS  : policy-traces.kql step proxy-inbound shows mcpMethod initialize, the organization,
#         the user's oid and the client app id (azp) of the token.
# @name initAuth
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP 8 - notifications/initialized
# EXPECT: HTTP 202, empty body.
# @name initialized
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notifications/initialized"}

###
# STEP 9 - tools/list
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE with 26 read-only tools (X-MCP-Readonly: true from mcp-backend-headers).
# @name toolsList
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"}

###
# STEP 10 - tools/call (read-only)
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE. data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"[{\"id\":\"<project-id>\",\"name\":\"<project>\",...}]"}]},"id":3,...}
#         The tool-validation fragment checked the arguments first (trace result "passed").
# LOGS  : tool-validation.kql (result passed, reachedBackend true).
# @name toolsCall
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"core_list_projects","arguments":{"top":5}}}

###
# STEP 11 - tools/call with an invalid argument (rejected by the gateway)
# WHAT  : top is outside the curated schema (1-100). More cases: 03-tool-validation.http.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, Content-Type: application/json (not SSE: the gateway answers itself),
#         header x-mcp-gateway-validation: rejected, body
#           {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"Invalid arguments for tool
#            core_list_projects: top violates maximum. Rejected by the API gateway before reaching
#            the server.","data":{"tool":"core_list_projects","violations":[{"field":"top","rule":"maximum"}],"source":"api-gateway"}}}
#         Compare native step 11: there the server simply accepts top: 500.
# LOGS  : tool-validation.kql (result rejected, reachedBackend false).
# @name toolsCallInvalid
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{bearer}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"core_list_projects","arguments":{"top":500}}}
